      {
        "claim": "Opposite-sign integer anchors reduce to {-1,1}",
        "location": "Auxiliary results L1.",
        "hypotheses": "Two distinct nonzero integer elements have opposite signs, and their product plus one is a rational square.",
        "reasoning": "Their integer product is at most -1. Since the product plus one is nonnegative, the product is at least -1 and therefore equals -1; the pair is {-1,1}. Negating every element preserves pairwise products, so the other integer-anchor case can be represented by positive integer anchors, without implying that the remaining elements are positive. For any additional element x paired with both exceptional anchors, x+1 and 1-x are nonnegative squares; hence -1 ≤ x ≤ 1, and distinctness from the anchors makes both inequalities strict. There is no third distinct nonzero integer in that interval.",
        "conclusion": "The sign reduction and the stated limitation on the signs of the other elements follow. They do not decide whether four additional rational elements can satisfy all required edges."
      },
      {
        "claim": "Proposed target A: denominator and divisibility conclusions for common neighbors of {-1,1}",
        "location": "Snapshot inline artifact, proposed target A; the parametrization is also given in its historical reduction paragraph.",
        "hypotheses": "For primitive integers m,n with n>0, m≠0 and m≠±n, set q=m²+n² and F(m/n)=4mn(n²-m²)/q².",
        "reasoning": "For every odd prime p dividing q, p divides neither m nor n. If p divided n²-m² as well, it would divide 2m² and hence m, a contradiction. Thus no odd prime factor of q cancels against the numerator. If q is even, primitiveness forces m,n odd; q≡2 mod 8 and n²-m² is divisible by 8, so the only cancellation at 2 against q² is a factor 4. The reduced denominator is therefore (q/gcd(q,2))². Every odd prime p dividing q has -1 as a square modulo p, since (m/n)²≡-1 mod p, so p≡1 mod 4. The positive square root q/gcd(q,2) is at least 5: the only possible smaller q for nonzero primitive m,n is q=2, which has m=±n and is excluded. The numerator is divisible by 24. For divisibility by 8, if q is odd then m,n have opposite parity and mn is even; if q is even, m,n are odd and n²-m² is divisible by 8. For divisibility by 3, either 3 divides mn or the nonzero squares m²,n² agree modulo 3. In the even-q case cancellation by 4 leaves a numerator divisible by 8, and q is not divisible by 3, so the reduced numerator remains divisible by 24.",
        "conclusion": "The stated denominator formula, lower bound 25, odd-prime restriction on its square root, and reduced-numerator divisibility by 24 hold for the specified primitive parameters and either sign of m."
      },
      {
        "claim": "Proposed target A: scope of the claimed prime restrictions",
        "location": "Snapshot inline artifact, proposed target A.",
        "hypotheses": "The proposal calls the numerator/denominator prime restrictions “exact,” but specifically states the denominator formula and odd-prime condition, plus numerator divisibility by 24.",
        "conclusion": "Those specific statements are verified above. No additional numerator prime-factor classification is precise enough in the supplied text to audit or conclude. These per-neighbor restrictions do not establish the six mutual square conditions among four additional elements."
      },
      {
        "claim": "Proposed target B: fixed finite local square tests do not obstruct the {-1,1} candidate family",
        "location": "Snapshot inline artifact, proposed target B.",
        "hypotheses": "Let S be any finite set of primes, P its product (P=1 if S is empty), and M=P. Choose arbitrarily large positive L≡1 mod P and set t_i=iP/L for i=1,2,3,4. Take L sufficiently large that the t_i lie in an interval (0,ε) where F(t)=4t(1-t²)/(1+t²)² is strictly increasing and 0<F(t)<1.",
        "reasoning": "There are infinitely many such L. Strict increase makes the four x_i=F(t_i) distinct and in (0,1); as L increases in this range, x_1 strictly decreases, giving infinitely many distinct sets. The identities 1+F(t)=((t²-2t-1)/(1+t²))² and 1-F(t)=((1-2t-t²)/(1+t²))² give rational-square edges from every x_i to both anchors; the edge between -1 and 1 has value zero, also a rational square. For p∈S, L is a p-adic unit and v_p(t_i)≥1. The factors 1±t_i² are units, so v_p(x_i)≥1 for odd p and v_2(x_i)=2+v_2(t_i)≥3 for p=2. Consequently v_p(x_ix_j)≥1 for odd p and v_2(x_ix_j)≥3. For odd p, 1+y with v_p(y)≥1 has a square root in Q_p by Hensel lifting the simple root 1 modulo p. For p=2, v_2(y)≥3 gives 1+y≡1 mod 8; every such unit is a square in Q_2. One elementary lifting argument starts with an odd root modulo 8 and, at each stage k≥3, adjusts a root modulo 2^k by either 0 or 2^(k-1) to match the next bit modulo 2^(k+1). Applying these facts to y=x_ix_j proves the six remaining edge values are squares in every Q_p for p∈S.",
        "conclusion": "For any fixed finite S, infinitely many distinct candidates with anchors {-1,1} satisfy the global rational-square anchor edges and the six remaining edges are squares in every Q_p for p∈S. This is a finite-local-test barrier only: it does not give rational square roots for those six edges."
      },
